{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.IwahoriHecke.AvgCrossing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.AvgCrossing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.IwahoriHecke.Quotient","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NoncommRing"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.AvgCrossing.T_avg_centered_squared_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.AvgCrossing.T_avg_centered_squared_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/AvgCrossing.lean#L116-L124","doc":"**Corollary** — the centered Avg-crossing element\n`T_s − (q − 1)/2` is \"scalar up to factor 2\".  Stated here in\na form that avoids division so it works over any `CommRing R`:\n\n  `(2 · T_s − (q − 1))² − (q + 1)² = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.AvgCrossing.T_avg_squares_to_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.AvgCrossing.T_avg_squares_to_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/AvgCrossing.lean#L68-L114","doc":"**Foundational identity: scaled averaged crossing squares to a scalar.**\n\nFor every generator `T_s` of `IwahoriHeckeQuot R q S` (the\nHecke-quadratic-only quotient), the algebraic identity\n\n  `(2 · T_s − (q − 1))² = (q + 1)²`\n\nholds.  This is the scaled form of the \"`σ − h/2` squares to\na scalar\" Clifford-like identity for inter-nucleon Avg\ncrossings in the canonical atom-braid sub-algebra of\n`H_{3A}(q)`; in the standard form `(T − q)(T + 1) = 0` the\nscalar `h = q − 1` (not `q − q⁻¹`, which would require the\nsymmetric form `(T − q)(T + q⁻¹) = 0`).\n\nProof: LHS − RHS factors as `4 · (T − q)(T + 1)`, which the\nHecke relation sends to `4 · 0 = 0`.  Both sides expand to\n`4·T² + (linear in T) + const`; using `T² = (q − 1)·T + q`\nfrom the Hecke relation eliminates the `T²` term, the\nlinear-in-`T` coefficients cancel, and the constant-term\nidentity `4q + (q − 1)² = (q + 1)²` is `ring`-true.\n\nNote the expansion crosses `T·q̂` against `q̂·T`, so it is *not*\navailable to `linear_combination`, which needs a `CommSemiring`\n— and `IwahoriHeckeQuot` is deliberately non-commutative.  What\nmakes the identity go through is narrower: `q̂ = algebraMap R _ q`\nis **central** (`Algebra.commute_algebraMap_left`), so the two\ncross terms merge even though the ambient algebra does not\ncommute. ","signatureChunk":0}]}